  "text": "Some migratory fish develop scales that show a series of concentric ridges, and the count of these ridges rises as the fish grows larger. Each year of normal development leaves a distinct ring on the scale that can be divided into two zones: one where the ridges are spaced relatively far apart, and another where they lie much closer together. The first zone is believed to be produced while the animal lives in the ocean, and the second while it resides in brackish or freshwater habitats; roughly, they correspond to the warm and cold seasons. The cause of the seasonal change in ridge spacing is still unclear. It may stem from an internal biological rhythm that is little affected by the environment, or it could be driven by external environmental fluctuations. Despite the commercial importance of this knowledge, experimental evidence remains scarce."
